<p>What follows is reproduced in John's own words, exactly as he wrote them — nothing edited, softened, or left out. He wrote in a broad, joking, tall-tale style typical of local-color memoir writing from his generation, playing up a folksy, small-town-goofball persona that was entirely a put-on: he was an articulate, well-read man, and the voice on the page is a deliberate comic choice, not a reflection of how he actually wrote or spoke. Where a spelling or typing slip appears in the text below, it's almost certainly a transcription error from retyping the original typescript into digital form, rather than an error of John's — he wasn't known to make them. He also did not write about Creede's mining-camp characters, or about race, gender, or the people around him, the way anyone would write about them today. Where a piece needs a specific content note beyond the general one above, it carries its own.</p>

<p>John signed his newspaper column and several self-published books with the pen name "Thundermug." Some of the poems reproduced here are signed and dated that way in the original.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>From the manuscript</h2>

  <p><em>Prologue to the Preface</em></p>

  <p><em>"Hey there, don't skip this page. If you want to go hell, you must read all of the publication regardless of its contents or how boring it may seem."</em></p>

  <p>Before you turn another page, however, be advised that the authors are not writers, per se. We are mentally unstable due to many and varied factors and grossly uninhibited in our advanced years. Pauline Roberta Jackson Bates, known affectionately as "Bubbles," has a degree from a prestigious college that qualifies her as a learned person and capable of writing real novels. Her ambitions along that line were rudely interrupted when she was kidnapped by aliens from a distant galaxy just after graduation. She was returned with a vast knowledge of the universe and interstellar travel but her brain was wiped clean of prior academic learning. My credentials are far less noteworthy, though I have exposed my instability and incompetence in other publications that were well received in Latvia, where a friend on missionary work shared them with students in his classes. They couldn't read them, but they liked the illustrations.</p>

  <p>In our immediate family, Bubbles and I have outlasted the other players and share a common bond in spite of our disparate mental abilities. Our goal, in this publication, is to jog memories of oldsters and acquaint others with short stories of a variety of people who influenced us, one way or another, and gave the mining town of Creede the aura and flavor that pervades to this day and draws the curious into its fold.</p>
</section>

